    "final_text": "I've built the todo CLI. All 66 tests pass with `npm test`, and I also ran every command by hand in a temporary directory to check the real output.\n\n**Files**\n- `index.js` \u2013 the command-line part: reads the arguments, prints results and sets the exit code.\n- `todos.js` \u2013 loads and saves `todos.json` and does the add, complete and delete work.\n- `package.json` \u2013 `npm test` runs the tests with Node's built-in test runner. There are no dependencies.\n- `test/todos.test.js` \u2013 unit tests for `todos.js`.\n- `test/cli.test.js` \u2013 end-to-end tests that run `node index.js` in a fresh temporary directory for each test.\n- `README.md` \u2013 short usage notes. I also added `todos.json` to `.gitignore`.\n\n**Example**\n```\n$ node index.js list\n1. [x] Buy groceries\n2. [ ] Walk the dog\n```\n\n**Edge cases handled**\n- **Missing text or id:** the command exits with status 1 and prints a message showing correct usage.\n- **Invalid ids:** values like `abc`, `0`, `-1`, `1.5`, `1e2` or several ids at once are rejected.\n- **Unknown ids:** you get `Todo #N not found`.\n- **Empty list:** `list` prints a \"No todos yet\" message.\n- **Unknown or missing command:** the help text is printed.\n\nAll errors go to stderr as one line (no stack trace) with exit status 1.\n\n**Assumptions I made**\n- **`todos.json` is a plain JSON array** of todos. A new todo's id is the highest existing id plus one, so deleting a todo never renumbers the others. The catch is that if you delete the todo with the highest id, the next todo you add gets that id again. If ids must never be reused, the file would need to store a counter as well, which means changing its format.\n- **Unquoted words are joined:** `add Buy some milk` works the same as `add \"Buy some milk\"`. Spaces at the start and end of the text are removed.\n- **Completing a todo that's already done** prints a notice and exits with status 0 instead of failing.\n- **A corrupted `todos.json` is left alone:** the command reports an error and never overwrites the file. Saves are written to a temporary file first and then swapped in, so an interrupted write can't cut the file short.\n\nI haven't committed anything; the changes are in the working tree for you to review.",
